	{"kanji": "大江山", "name": "Mount Ōe", "from": 30, "note": "Mount Ōe was the stronghold of the oni king Shuten-dōji, who in legend was slain by Minamoto no Raikō and his four retainers, Watanabe no Tsuna among them, after they drugged his wine."},
	{"kanji": "那須野", "name": "Nasu", "from": 40, "note": "On Nasu moor stands the Sesshō-seki, the Killing Stone, said to be the body of the nine-tailed fox Tamamo-no-Mae. Tradition says the monk Gennō split it in 1385."},
	{"kanji": "雪国", "name": "Snow Country", "from": 50, "note": "The snow country lies along the Japan Sea, buried each winter. There the Yuki-onna, the snow woman, is said to appear to travellers lost in blizzards."},
	{"kanji": "河童淵", "name": "Kappa Pool", "from": 60, "note": "Kappa-buchi is a stream at Tōno in the north, long said to be full of kappa. They love cucumbers and sumo, and lose their strength if the water in the dish on their head is spilled."},
	{"kanji": "化野", "name": "Adashino", "from": 70, "note": "Adashino, west of Kyoto, was a burial ground where the dead were once left in the open. Some eight thousand stone markers for the unmourned now stand at Adashino Nenbutsu-ji."},
	{"kanji": "恐山", "name": "Mount Osore", "from": 80, "note": "Osore-zan, in the far north, is one of Japan's three great sacred mountains: a sulphurous caldera held to be an entrance to the afterlife, with its own Sanzu River to cross."},
	{"kanji": "百鬼夜行", "name": "Night Parade", "from": 90, "note": "Hyakki Yagyō, the night parade of a hundred demons, was dreaded in Heian Kyoto. On the nights it was expected, the nobility stayed indoors rather than meet it."},
	{"kanji": "禅寺", "name": "Zen Temple", "from": 100, "note": "Zen took root in Japan in the 12th and 13th centuries with the monks Eisai and Dōgen. A kōan is a question set by a teacher that reasoning alone cannot answer."},
	{"kanji": "石庭", "name": "Stone Garden", "from": 118, "note": "The dry garden, karesansui, uses raked gravel for water and stones for mountains. The most famous, at Ryōan-ji in Kyoto, has fifteen stones laid so that one is always hidden from view."},
	{"kanji": "鳥居", "name": "The Gate", "from": 136, "note": "A torii marks the boundary between the everyday world and sacred ground at a Shinto shrine. At Fushimi Inari in Kyoto, thousands of vermilion gates line the mountain path."},
	{"kanji": "墨染", "name": "Wet Ink", "from": 154, "note": "Ink painting came to Japan with Zen. Its masters, among them Sesshū (1420–1506), whose Long Scroll hangs above this road, prized the stroke that cannot be corrected."},
	{"kanji": "円相", "name": "Ensō", "from": 172, "note": "The ensō is a circle brushed in a single movement by Zen monks, both as practice and as a portrait of the mind at that moment."},
	{"kanji": "硯", "name": "Ink Stone", "from": 190, "note": "Brush, ink, paper and inkstone are the Four Treasures of the Study. Ink is a solid stick, ground with water on the stone before each sitting, and the grinding itself was held to settle the mind."},
	{"kanji": "無刀", "name": "No Sword", "from": 208, "note": "Mutō, no sword, was the pride of the Yagyū Shinkage school: disarming a swordsman with bare hands. Yagyū Muneyoshi showed it to Tokugawa Ieyasu in 1594, and his family became sword teachers to the shoguns."},
	{"kanji": "達磨", "name": "Daruma", "from": 226, "note": "Daruma dolls are modelled on Bodhidharma, who carried Zen from India to China and is said to have sat facing a wall for nine years. Weighted to right themselves, they are sold without eyes: one is painted in when a wish is made, the other when it comes true."},
	{"kanji": "狸", "name": "Tanuki", "from": 244, "note": "The tanuki, or raccoon dog, is a real animal and a famous shapeshifter of folklore, changing form with a leaf on its head. In the tale Bunbuku Chagama one turns itself into a tea kettle at the temple Morin-ji."},
	{"kanji": "赤い糸", "name": "Red Thread", "from": 262, "note": "Hitodama are pale balls of fire said to be souls leaving the body. The red thread of fate, a belief shared across East Asia, joins two people who are destined to meet."},
	{"kanji": "風", "name": "Wind", "from": 280, "note": "Typhoons destroyed the Mongol invasion fleets of 1274 and 1281 off Kyushu. The storms were named kamikaze, the divine wind."},
	{"kanji": "雨", "name": "Rain", "from": 298, "note": "Tsuyu, the plum rains, soak Japan for about six weeks each early summer. Rivers rise, and in the old stories the kappa rise with them."},
	{"kanji": "雪", "name": "Deep Snow", "from": 316, "note": "The snow country along the Japan Sea can lie under several metres each winter. The poet Issa, who was born and died in it, wrote: so this is my last home? Five feet of snow."},
	{"kanji": "夜", "name": "Night", "from": 334, "note": "Before electric light, travellers carried paper lanterns painted with their house crest. In the game Hyakumonogatari a hundred ghost stories were told through the night, and one light put out after each."},
	{"kanji": "死神", "name": "Shinigami", "from": 352, "note": "The shinigami, a spirit that draws people toward death, appears in Edo-period puppet plays about lovers' suicides and in the 1841 picture book Ehon Hyaku Monogatari."},
	{"kanji": "式神", "name": "Shikigami", "from": 370, "note": "Onmyōdō, the Way of Yin and Yang, was the court science of divination and protection in Heian Japan. Its most famous master, Abe no Seimei (921–1005), is said to have commanded twelve shikigami."},
